\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S1}{\ignorespaces Design of the four main campaigns. Active technical traders responded to the quoted price; held technical traders stayed in Hold. All dealers had finite reserves. The size campaign used active technical traders at dealer depth $h=1$ and $100$, and held technical traders at $h=100$. Counts include newly simulated no-switch controls, with one such control shared across fractions within a starting market and condition where the design permits.}}{17}{table.caption.25}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{tab:campaigns}{{S1}{17}{Design of the four main campaigns. Active technical traders responded to the quoted price; held technical traders stayed in Hold. All dealers had finite reserves. The size campaign used active technical traders at dealer depth $h=1$ and $100$, and held technical traders at $h=100$. Counts include newly simulated no-switch controls, with one such control shared across fractions within a starting market and condition where the design permits}{table.caption.25}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S2}{\ignorespaces Paired rule switches on a $64\times 64$ grid over 3,000 post-switch steps. In A, majority probability $p$ counts paths reaching an interior Sell fraction of at least one-half; median onset is conditional on reaching that value. Undefined means no run reached a majority. In B, DD is median maximum drawdown in percent. $\Delta $DD is the median paired difference, in percentage points, with a pointwise 95\% whole-market bootstrap confidence interval (CI). These contrasts separate the rule switch from baseline financial variation.}}{17}{table.caption.26}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:paired-switch}{{S2}{17}{Paired rule switches on a $64\times 64$ grid over 3,000 post-switch steps. In A, majority probability $p$ counts paths reaching an interior Sell fraction of at least one-half; median onset is conditional on reaching that value. Undefined means no run reached a majority. In B, DD is median maximum drawdown in percent. $\Delta $DD is the median paired difference, in percentage points, with a pointwise 95\% whole-market bootstrap confidence interval (CI). These contrasts separate the rule switch from baseline financial variation}{table.caption.26}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S3}{\ignorespaces Grid-size comparisons over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Brackets give 95\% confidence intervals (CI) from 2,000 whole-market bootstrap samples. $n_{\rm  int}$ counts fractions with $0.05<\hat  p<0.95$. Crossings use first upward linear interpolation. Susceptibility maxima are selected over the sampled grid, including reselection in each bootstrap sample. Undefined denotes an unbracketed crossing; intervals use available bracketed samples.}}{17}{table.caption.27}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:finite-size-scaling}{{S3}{17}{Grid-size comparisons over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Brackets give 95\% confidence intervals (CI) from 2,000 whole-market bootstrap samples. $n_{\rm int}$ counts fractions with $0.05<\hat p<0.95$. Crossings use first upward linear interpolation. Susceptibility maxima are selected over the sampled grid, including reselection in each bootstrap sample. Undefined denotes an unbracketed crossing; intervals use available bracketed samples}{table.caption.27}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S4}{\ignorespaces Probability of a Sell majority after changes in trader cash and shares over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Each fraction includes 27 combinations of cash, shares, and dealer depth. Held decisions gave the same probability across those combinations; active decisions gave the stated range.}}{18}{table.caption.28}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:balance-fractions}{{S4}{18}{Probability of a Sell majority after changes in trader cash and shares over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Each fraction includes 27 combinations of cash, shares, and dealer depth. Held decisions gave the same probability across those combinations; active decisions gave the stated range}{table.caption.28}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S5}{\ignorespaces A one-time instruction to sell versus a persistent rule switch at the same selected interior traders. Both conditions used dealer depth $h=100$, 3,000 post-intervention steps, and no-switch controls. Entries are majority probabilities with 95\% Wilson intervals.}}{18}{table.caption.29}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-pulse}{{S5}{18}{A one-time instruction to sell versus a persistent rule switch at the same selected interior traders. Both conditions used dealer depth $h=100$, 3,000 post-intervention steps, and no-switch controls. Entries are majority probabilities with 95\% Wilson intervals}{table.caption.29}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S6}{\ignorespaces All single-response changes to Rule~19280 with dealer depth $h=100$. Each entry gives majority probability with technical traders held / active over 20 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Outputs are Buy $=0$, Hold $=1$, and Sell $=2$. This descriptive screen used no multiplicity adjustment.}}{18}{table.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-rules}{{S6}{18}{All single-response changes to Rule~19280 with dealer depth $h=100$. Each entry gives majority probability with technical traders held / active over 20 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Outputs are Buy $=0$, Hold $=1$, and Sell $=2$. This descriptive screen used no multiplicity adjustment}{table.caption.30}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S7}{\ignorespaces Adjusting versus fixed Hold-band widths at fraction $f=0.175$ over 50 starting markets. Brackets in the rows give 95\% Wilson confidence intervals (CI). Sell is the mean fraction of technical traders in Sell over post-switch steps and starting markets. The moving center continued to update. The probability at dealer depth $h=0.1$ minus that at $h=100$ was -0.06 with adaptive widths and -0.26 with frozen widths. Their paired difference was 0.20, with a 95\% interval [-0.08, 0.50] from 2,000 whole-market bootstrap samples.}}{19}{table.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-band}{{S7}{19}{Adjusting versus fixed Hold-band widths at fraction $f=0.175$ over 50 starting markets. Brackets in the rows give 95\% Wilson confidence intervals (CI). Sell is the mean fraction of technical traders in Sell over post-switch steps and starting markets. The moving center continued to update. The probability at dealer depth $h=0.1$ minus that at $h=100$ was -0.06 with adaptive widths and -0.26 with frozen widths. Their paired difference was 0.20, with a 95\% interval [-0.08, 0.50] from 2,000 whole-market bootstrap samples}{table.caption.31}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S8}{\ignorespaces Additional dealer-depth comparisons at fraction $f=0.20$ with active technical traders and 50 starting markets over 3,000 post-switch steps. The initial log-price slope per normalized net purchase is approximately $2/h$; this is implied by the reserve curve, not a separate price-impact coefficient. Depth changes funding and price sensitivity together. Drawdown is a percentage, and the dealer-rejection fraction uses post-switch eligible orders. Probability brackets are 95\% Wilson intervals.}}{19}{table.caption.32}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-impact}{{S8}{19}{Additional dealer-depth comparisons at fraction $f=0.20$ with active technical traders and 50 starting markets over 3,000 post-switch steps. The initial log-price slope per normalized net purchase is approximately $2/h$; this is implied by the reserve curve, not a separate price-impact coefficient. Depth changes funding and price sensitivity together. Drawdown is a percentage, and the dealer-rejection fraction uses post-switch eligible orders. Probability brackets are 95\% Wilson intervals}{table.caption.32}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S9}{\ignorespaces Observation time and the required Sell fraction with technical traders held and dealer depth $h=100$. Entries are interpolated $f_{50}$ with 95\% whole-market bootstrap intervals over 50 starting markets. The fraction grid is independent of adaptive refinement. Undefined denotes an unbracketed crossing; intervals use available bracketed samples.}}{19}{table.caption.33}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-horizons}{{S9}{19}{Observation time and the required Sell fraction with technical traders held and dealer depth $h=100$. Entries are interpolated $f_{50}$ with 95\% whole-market bootstrap intervals over 50 starting markets. The fraction grid is independent of adaptive refinement. Undefined denotes an unbracketed crossing; intervals use available bracketed samples}{table.caption.33}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S10}{\ignorespaces Logistic fits and sampled susceptibility maxima over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Parentheses give whole-market bootstrap standard errors (SE). The fitted curve imposes increasing probability; empirical crossings do not. The final column gives a sampled fraction, whose precision is limited by the local fraction grid.}}{20}{table.caption.34}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-fits}{{S10}{20}{Logistic fits and sampled susceptibility maxima over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. Parentheses give whole-market bootstrap standard errors (SE). The fitted curve imposes increasing probability; empirical crossings do not. The final column gives a sampled fraction, whose precision is limited by the local fraction grid}{table.caption.34}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S11}{\ignorespaces Longer observation with dealer depth $h=100$ and active technical traders. Times are steps after the rule switch, following 1,000 baseline steps. Drawdowns and price changes are percentages; price changes use the quoted price at the rule switch as reference. Final Sell averages the last 500 steps of the longer observation period. None means no Sell majority by 10,000 steps. Selection used newly computed short-run summaries before inspection of images. The extensions follow the same starting markets, and are not independent replications.}}{20}{table.caption.35}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:market-panel-horizons}{{S11}{20}{Longer observation with dealer depth $h=100$ and active technical traders. Times are steps after the rule switch, following 1,000 baseline steps. Drawdowns and price changes are percentages; price changes use the quoted price at the rule switch as reference. Final Sell averages the last 500 steps of the longer observation period. None means no Sell majority by 10,000 steps. Selection used newly computed short-run summaries before inspection of images. The extensions follow the same starting markets, and are not independent replications}{table.caption.35}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S12}{\ignorespaces Transaction-fee comparisons. Each condition used 50 starting markets. The fee was charged to each completed buyer and seller from initialization. Sell-majority probabilities are followed by Wilson 95\% intervals. In Panel A, drawdown and cumulative dealer fees are medians for the switched runs at fraction $f=0.20$. Drawdown uses the 3,000-step post-switch observation period, including the quoted price immediately before the switch. Fees include all 4,000 trading steps and are expressed as a percentage of initial aggregate trader cash. Fee income was held separately from trading reserves. Panel B reports paired differences between 25 and zero basis points with percentile 95\% intervals from 2,000 whole-market bootstrap samples. The probability difference is a difference in proportions; drawdown differences are median paired changes in percentage points. The complete protocol, all six tested fractions, and paired differences are archived with the transaction-fee study.}}{21}{table.caption.36}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:transaction-fees}{{S12}{21}{Transaction-fee comparisons. Each condition used 50 starting markets. The fee was charged to each completed buyer and seller from initialization. Sell-majority probabilities are followed by Wilson 95\% intervals. In Panel A, drawdown and cumulative dealer fees are medians for the switched runs at fraction $f=0.20$. Drawdown uses the 3,000-step post-switch observation period, including the quoted price immediately before the switch. Fees include all 4,000 trading steps and are expressed as a percentage of initial aggregate trader cash. Fee income was held separately from trading reserves. Panel B reports paired differences between 25 and zero basis points with percentile 95\% intervals from 2,000 whole-market bootstrap samples. The probability difference is a difference in proportions; drawdown differences are median paired changes in percentage points. The complete protocol, all six tested fractions, and paired differences are archived with the transaction-fee study}{table.caption.36}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The changed response and paired market decisions. \textbf  {(A)}~Baseline Rule~19280. \textbf  {(B)}~Rule~19286 after the rule switch. Each cross contains the four neighbors used to determine the next decision, shown below its arrow. The hollow center is the trader being updated; its current decision does not enter the lookup table. Blue, gray, and orange denote Buy~(0), Hold~(1), and Sell~(2). One arrangement represents each neighbor sum; every arrangement with that sum produces the same response. The outlined column is the only response the rule switch changes: three Buys and one Hold produce Buy under the baseline and Sell after the switch. \textbf  {(C)}~Paired $64\times 64$ grids at the rule switch and after 100, 400, and 3,000 steps. The upper row retains the baseline rule; the lower assigns the new rule to 20\% of interior traders. Both paths share a 1,000-step baseline, dealer depth $h=100$, and active technical traders. Starting market 14 was selected from 50 by proximity to the median paired difference in peak Sell fraction. Ensemble outcomes and conditional onset times appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:paired-switch}.}}{22}{figure.caption.38}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rule-quench}{{1}{22}{The changed response and paired market decisions. \textbf {(A)}~Baseline Rule~19280. \textbf {(B)}~Rule~19286 after the rule switch. Each cross contains the four neighbors used to determine the next decision, shown below its arrow. The hollow center is the trader being updated; its current decision does not enter the lookup table. Blue, gray, and orange denote Buy~(0), Hold~(1), and Sell~(2). One arrangement represents each neighbor sum; every arrangement with that sum produces the same response. The outlined column is the only response the rule switch changes: three Buys and one Hold produce Buy under the baseline and Sell after the switch. \textbf {(C)}~Paired $64\times 64$ grids at the rule switch and after 100, 400, and 3,000 steps. The upper row retains the baseline rule; the lower assigns the new rule to 20\% of interior traders. Both paths share a 1,000-step baseline, dealer depth $h=100$, and active technical traders. Starting market 14 was selected from 50 by proximity to the median paired difference in peak Sell fraction. Ensemble outcomes and conditional onset times appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:paired-switch}}{figure.caption.38}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Rule switches across dealer depths over 3,000 post-switch steps. \textbf  {(A)}~Probability of an interior Sell majority with technical traders held or active. Bands are 95\% Wilson intervals over 50 starting markets. \textbf  {(B)}~Active-minus-held majority probability at each depth and fraction. \textbf  {(C)}~Median maximum drawdown at fraction $f=0.20$, with interquartile bands. \textbf  {(D)}~Median fraction of post-switch eligible orders rejected at fraction $f=0.20$, on a logarithmic axis with interquartile bands. Dealer depth $h$ is initial dealer shares per trader and also determines price sensitivity through the reserve curve. The dashed overlay in A at dealer depth $h=10$ is a distinct finite-depth comparison; the deep arm is dealer depth $h=100$. At zero dealer depth only opposing trader orders match and price stays fixed. Panels C and D describe switched markets. No-switch control drawdowns and paired differences appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:paired-switch}.}}{23}{figure.caption.39}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:reservoir}{{2}{23}{Rule switches across dealer depths over 3,000 post-switch steps. \textbf {(A)}~Probability of an interior Sell majority with technical traders held or active. Bands are 95\% Wilson intervals over 50 starting markets. \textbf {(B)}~Active-minus-held majority probability at each depth and fraction. \textbf {(C)}~Median maximum drawdown at fraction $f=0.20$, with interquartile bands. \textbf {(D)}~Median fraction of post-switch eligible orders rejected at fraction $f=0.20$, on a logarithmic axis with interquartile bands. Dealer depth $h$ is initial dealer shares per trader and also determines price sensitivity through the reserve curve. The dashed overlay in A at dealer depth $h=10$ is a distinct finite-depth comparison; the deep arm is dealer depth $h=100$. At zero dealer depth only opposing trader orders match and price stays fixed. Panels C and D describe switched markets. No-switch control drawdowns and paired differences appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:paired-switch}}{figure.caption.39}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Market size and the formation of a Sell majority over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. \textbf  {(A)}~Majority probability with technical traders held, with 95\% Wilson bands. \textbf  {(B)}~Susceptibility with technical traders held: the variance across markets of the final 500-step mean Sell fraction, multiplied by the number of interior traders. \textbf  {(C)}~Interpolated fraction $f_{50}(L)$ at which half the markets reached a majority, against inverse grid size, with technical traders held at dealer depth $h=100$ and active at $h=1$ and $h=100$. \textbf  {(D)}~Sampled maximum susceptibility versus grid size. Error bars in C and D are 95\% whole-market bootstrap intervals. Solid lines connect tested values; dashed curves in A are logistic fits. No law for indefinitely large grids was fitted.}}{24}{figure.caption.40}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:finite-size}{{3}{24}{Market size and the formation of a Sell majority over 50 starting markets and 3,000 post-switch steps. \textbf {(A)}~Majority probability with technical traders held, with 95\% Wilson bands. \textbf {(B)}~Susceptibility with technical traders held: the variance across markets of the final 500-step mean Sell fraction, multiplied by the number of interior traders. \textbf {(C)}~Interpolated fraction $f_{50}(L)$ at which half the markets reached a majority, against inverse grid size, with technical traders held at dealer depth $h=100$ and active at $h=1$ and $h=100$. \textbf {(D)}~Sampled maximum susceptibility versus grid size. Error bars in C and D are 95\% whole-market bootstrap intervals. Solid lines connect tested values; dashed curves in A are logistic fits. No law for indefinitely large grids was fitted}{figure.caption.40}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Longer histories of five numerically selected markets with active technical traders and dealer depth $h=100$. Blue, gray, and orange denote Buy, Hold, and Sell. \textbf  {(A)}~Grid decisions at the rule switch and after 1,500, 3,000, 5,000, and 10,000 steps. Selection used the newly computed 3,000-step summaries before inspection of images. At that endpoint, the first two markets had not reached a majority and the other three had. Nonmajority examples were nearest their group's median final Sell fraction; majority examples were nearest their group's median onset, with the starting-market number breaking ties. Starting markets were 2, 15, 41, 5, and 38, from left to right. The longer outcomes did not affect selection. \textbf  {(B)}~Quoted price divided by its value at the rule switch. \textbf  {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction, with one-half marked by a horizontal line. The dark vertical dashed line marks the original 3,000-step endpoint; other guides mark the remaining snapshots. Onsets and financial summaries appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:market-panel-horizons}.}}{25}{figure.caption.41}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dose-panels}{{4}{25}{Longer histories of five numerically selected markets with active technical traders and dealer depth $h=100$. Blue, gray, and orange denote Buy, Hold, and Sell. \textbf {(A)}~Grid decisions at the rule switch and after 1,500, 3,000, 5,000, and 10,000 steps. Selection used the newly computed 3,000-step summaries before inspection of images. At that endpoint, the first two markets had not reached a majority and the other three had. Nonmajority examples were nearest their group's median final Sell fraction; majority examples were nearest their group's median onset, with the starting-market number breaking ties. Starting markets were 2, 15, 41, 5, and 38, from left to right. The longer outcomes did not affect selection. \textbf {(B)}~Quoted price divided by its value at the rule switch. \textbf {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction, with one-half marked by a horizontal line. The dark vertical dashed line marks the original 3,000-step endpoint; other guides mark the remaining snapshots. Onsets and financial summaries appear in Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:market-panel-horizons}}{figure.caption.41}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The same starting market at different dealer depths with fraction $f=0.20$. \textbf  {(A)}~Grid decisions at the rule switch and after 500, 1,500, and 3,000 steps. The first three columns have active technical traders at dealer depth $h=0.1$, $1$, and $100$; the last holds the technical traders at $h=100$. Starting market 2 was selected among markets reaching a majority at all three dealer depths with active technical traders by minimizing the summed distance from their depth-specific median onsets, with the starting-market number breaking ties. \textbf  {(B)}~Quoted price relative to the rule switch. \textbf  {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction. The held condition prevents price feedback into decisions while retaining full trading and price calculations. The selected paths illustrate the same initialization across conditions; ensemble probabilities and drawdowns appear in Fig.~\ref {fig:reservoir}.}}{26}{figure.caption.42}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dealer-panels}{{5}{26}{The same starting market at different dealer depths with fraction $f=0.20$. \textbf {(A)}~Grid decisions at the rule switch and after 500, 1,500, and 3,000 steps. The first three columns have active technical traders at dealer depth $h=0.1$, $1$, and $100$; the last holds the technical traders at $h=100$. Starting market 2 was selected among markets reaching a majority at all three dealer depths with active technical traders by minimizing the summed distance from their depth-specific median onsets, with the starting-market number breaking ties. \textbf {(B)}~Quoted price relative to the rule switch. \textbf {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction. The held condition prevents price feedback into decisions while retaining full trading and price calculations. The selected paths illustrate the same initialization across conditions; ensemble probabilities and drawdowns appear in Fig.~\ref {fig:reservoir}}{figure.caption.42}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Changing trader cash and shares at the rule switch, with active technical traders, fraction $f=0.18$, and 50 starting markets over 3,000 post-switch steps. In A--C, the nine resource combinations are grouped by cash multiplier, with share multiplier on the axis and a series for each dealer depth. \textbf  {(A)}~Majority probability. The dashed line and band give the held-condition probability and its 95\% Wilson interval, identical across financial conditions. \textbf  {(B)}~Median maximum drawdown on a logarithmic axis. \textbf  {(C)}~Median fraction of post-switch eligible orders rejected, on a logarithmic axis. \textbf  {(D)}~Majority probability against median drawdown for all 27 active combinations, with the held reference repeated. The drawdown axis follows B. Probability axes cover the full possible range; all displayed drawdowns and rejection medians are strictly positive.}}{27}{figure.caption.43}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:balance-sheet}{{6}{27}{Changing trader cash and shares at the rule switch, with active technical traders, fraction $f=0.18$, and 50 starting markets over 3,000 post-switch steps. In A--C, the nine resource combinations are grouped by cash multiplier, with share multiplier on the axis and a series for each dealer depth. \textbf {(A)}~Majority probability. The dashed line and band give the held-condition probability and its 95\% Wilson interval, identical across financial conditions. \textbf {(B)}~Median maximum drawdown on a logarithmic axis. \textbf {(C)}~Median fraction of post-switch eligible orders rejected, on a logarithmic axis. \textbf {(D)}~Majority probability against median drawdown for all 27 active combinations, with the held reference repeated. The drawdown axis follows B. Probability axes cover the full possible range; all displayed drawdowns and rejection medians are strictly positive}{figure.caption.43}{}}
